---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    { '@type': 'Question', 'name': 'Is SORBS still active?', 'acceptedAnswer': { '@type': 'Answer', 'text': 'No. Proofpoint, which owned the Spam and Open Relay Blocking System, decommissioned it on June 5, 2024, after more than two decades of operation. All SORBS zones, including dnsbl.sorbs.net, now return NXDOMAIN: the zone no longer exists in DNS. A SORBS listing can no longer exist, and delisting requests are no longer accepted.' } },
    { '@type': 'Question', 'name': 'How do I get delisted from SORBS?', 'acceptedAnswer': { '@type': 'Answer', 'text': 'You cannot, and you do not need to: the blocklist was shut down on June 5, 2024 and no longer exists. If a report or dashboard still shows a SORBS listing, the data predates the shutdown or the tool queries a dead zone. Run a current check against live blocklists instead, and fix the underlying cause if a live list has flagged your IP.' } },
    { '@type': 'Question', 'name': 'My mail server still queries SORBS zones. What should I do?', 'acceptedAnswer': { '@type': 'Answer', 'text': 'Remove every dnsbl.sorbs.net reference from your mail server configuration (Postfix, Exim, SpamAssassin, or any RBL plugin). Queries to the dead zone return NXDOMAIN, add needless DNS lookups and latency to every message, and provide zero filtering signal. Replace them with actively maintained lists such as Spamhaus ZEN, Barracuda, or SpamCop.' } },
    { '@type': 'Question', 'name': 'What replaced SORBS?', 'acceptedAnswer': { '@type': 'Answer', 'text': 'There is no one-to-one replacement, but the same ground is covered by actively maintained lists: Spamhaus ZEN (spam sources, exploits, dynamic IP policy), Barracuda (reputation), SpamCop (spamtrap-driven), and SpamRats (dynamic and generic IP space). Coverage of dynamic IP ranges, formerly the SORBS dul zone, is now handled by the Spamhaus PBL component of ZEN and by SpamRats.' } },
  ],
};
---
<BaseLayout title="SORBS Delisting: The Blocklist Was Shut Down in 2024 (What to Do Now)" description="SORBS was permanently decommissioned by Proofpoint on June 5, 2024. Delisting is no longer possible or necessary: how to confirm it, clean up dead zone queries, and re-baseline on live blocklists."
 breadcrumb={[["Home", "/"], ["Email Authentication", "/email-authentication/"], ["Email Deliverability", "/email-deliverability/"], ["SORBS Delisting"]]}>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/email-authentication/" class="hover:text-accent">Email Authentication</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/email-deliverability/" class="hover:text-accent">Email Deliverability</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">SORBS Delisting</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">SORBS Delisting: The Blocklist No Longer Exists</h1>
    <div class="pd-answer" data-vs="answer">
      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
        <strong class="text-ink">SORBS (Spam and Open-Relay Blocking System) was permanently decommissioned by its owner, Proofpoint, on June 5, 2024.</strong> Every SORBS zone, including <code>dnsbl.sorbs.net</code>, now returns NXDOMAIN - the domain no longer exists in DNS. A SORBS listing can no longer exist, so delisting is neither possible nor necessary. What you do instead depends on where you saw the SORBS reference, and the steps below cover each case.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="rich-text mt-8">
      <h2>Verified current status of the SORBS zones</h2>
      <p>
        We confirmed the shutdown by direct DNS query rather than by press release alone. The table below shows what every SORBS zone class returns today:
      </p>
      <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60">
        <table class="pdns-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Zone</th><th>What It Covered (Historical)</th><th>Current DNS Answer</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td class="font-mono text-xs text-ink">dnsbl.sorbs.net</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Composite spam blocklist (the zone most mail servers queried)</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">NXDOMAIN - zone gone from DNS</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="font-mono text-xs text-ink">spam.dnsbl.sorbs.net</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Spam sources and spam-run hosts</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">NXDOMAIN</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="font-mono text-xs text-ink">dul.dnsbl.sorbs.net</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Dynamic and non-static IP ranges</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">NXDOMAIN</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="font-mono text-xs text-ink">http.dnsbl.sorbs.net</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Open proxies and abused HTTP hosts</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">NXDOMAIN</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="font-mono text-xs text-ink">sorbs.net (parent domain)</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Zone parent and delisting web interface</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">NXDOMAIN - the whole domain is gone</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        The parent domain itself no longer resolves, which is stronger evidence than any zone-level test: there is no SORBS infrastructure left to file a delisting request with, and no nameserver left to answer a listing query. Anything that still reports a SORBS hit is reading stale data.
      </p>

      <h2>What to do instead of a SORBS delisting, step by step</h2>
      <p>Work through the steps that match where you encountered SORBS:</p>
      <ol class="list-decimal space-y-2 pl-5">
        <li><strong>Identify where the SORBS reference came from.</strong> A monitoring dashboard, an old audit report, or a mail-log line may all still mention SORBS. If a tool reports a SORBS listing today, that data predates the June 2024 shutdown or the tool queries a dead zone. Run a current scan with the <a href="/blacklist-checker/">blocklist checker</a> to see which live lists actually flag your IP.</li>
        <li><strong>Remove SORBS queries from your mail server configuration.</strong> Delete every <code>dnsbl.sorbs.net</code> reference from Postfix <code>postscreen_dnsbl_sites</code>, Exim RBLS lists, SpamAssassin <code>DNSEVAL</code> rules, or any RBL plugin. Dead-zone queries return NXDOMAIN, add needless DNS lookups and latency to every message, and provide zero filtering signal.</li>
        <li><strong>Re-baseline your sending IP against live blocklists.</strong> The lists that now cover SORBS's old ground are Spamhaus ZEN (spam sources, exploits, and the PBL dynamic-range policy), Barracuda, SpamCop, and SpamRats. The <a href="/ip-reputation-checker/">IP reputation checker</a> scans an IP against the publicly queryable ones in one pass.</li>
        <li><strong>Fix the deliverability posture the listing was warning about.</strong> The root causes SORBS tracked have not gone away: publish correct <a href="/spf-record-checker/">SPF</a> and <a href="/dkim-record-checker/">DKIM</a> records, enforce a <a href="/dmarc-record-checker/">DMARC</a> policy, set a matching PTR hostname, and never send directly from dynamic IP space.</li>
        <li><strong>If a live list flags you, use its own removal process.</strong> Delisting still works exactly as before on the lists that exist: see the <a href="/remove-from-spamhaus-blocklist/">Spamhaus removal</a> and <a href="/get-off-barracuda-blocklist/">Barracuda removal</a> guides for the two most common.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>How SORBS listings worked before the 2024 shutdown</h2>
      <p>
        The following is historical context, kept because it explains years of mail-server lore you may still encounter in documentation, old runbooks, and forum posts. SORBS listed IP addresses for spam-sending behavior, open relays, open proxies, and dynamic IP assignment. It was best known for two quirks: new IP space could appear on the dynamic-range list simply because of a past assignment, and some listing classes carried an escalation timer in which an incorrectly filed removal request could extend the wait rather than shorten it. None of that machinery exists anymore - the timers, the request forms, and the zones were all retired together on June 5, 2024.
      </p>
      <aside class="mt-6 rounded-xl border-l-3 border-l-violet/50 bg-violet/5 p-5" aria-label="Sysadmin field notes">
        <p class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.16em] text-violet mb-2">Sysadmin field notes</p>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">The SORBS shutdown is a quiet trap for inherited infrastructure. Mail servers configured years ago carry <code>dnsbl.sorbs.net</code> in their RBL lists, and because the dead zone answers NXDOMAIN instead of timing out, nothing visibly breaks - the checks just silently stop doing anything. If you inherit a mail server, grep its configuration for "sorbs" and remove the dead references: you get back the DNS lookup budget, and you stop mistaking a permanently-clear dead list for evidence that your IP reputation is good.</p>
      </aside>

      <h2>What we see across domains</h2>
      <p>
        In blocklist scans since the shutdown, SORBS entries have disappeared from live results while deliverability complaints that once blamed SORBS now trace to the lists that remain: Spamhaus ZEN and SpamCop dominate for spam-trap and compromised-account listings, and the dynamic-range PBL policy catches misconfigured new senders. The practical lesson of the SORBS era survives its subject: a blocklist hit is a symptom, and the cure is always fixing the sending source and authentication, not the listing.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Frequently asked questions</h2>
    <div class="mt-3 grid gap-3">
      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Is SORBS still active?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">No. Proofpoint, which owned the Spam and Open Relay Blocking System, decommissioned it on June 5, 2024, after more than two decades of operation. All SORBS zones, including dnsbl.sorbs.net, now return NXDOMAIN: the zone no longer exists in DNS. A SORBS listing can no longer exist, and delisting requests are no longer accepted.</p></details>
      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">How do I get delisted from SORBS?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">You cannot, and you do not need to: the blocklist was shut down on June 5, 2024 and no longer exists. If a report or dashboard still shows a SORBS listing, the data predates the shutdown or the tool queries a dead zone. Run a current check against live blocklists instead, and fix the underlying cause if a live list has flagged your IP.</p></details>
      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">My mail server still queries SORBS zones. What should I do?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Remove every dnsbl.sorbs.net reference from your mail server configuration (Postfix, Exim, SpamAssassin, or any RBL plugin). Queries to the dead zone return NXDOMAIN, add needless DNS lookups and latency to every message, and provide zero filtering signal. Replace them with actively maintained lists such as Spamhaus ZEN, Barracuda, or SpamCop.</p></details>
      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">What replaced SORBS?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">There is no one-to-one replacement, but the same ground is covered by actively maintained lists: Spamhaus ZEN (spam sources, exploits, dynamic IP policy), Barracuda (reputation), SpamCop (spamtrap-driven), and SpamRats (dynamic and generic IP space). Coverage of dynamic IP ranges, formerly the SORBS dul zone, is now handled by the Spamhaus PBL component of ZEN and by SpamRats.</p></details>
    </div>

    <h2>Next checks</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5">
      <li>Learn the wider delivery picture in <a href="/why-emails-go-to-spam/">why emails go to spam</a>.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
